arXiv:2510.02720v1 [eess.SY] 03 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wenjian Hao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zehui Lu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nicolas Miguel
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shaoshuai Mou
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>The authors are with the School of Aeronautics and Astronautics, Purdue University, West Lafayette, IN 47907, USA. <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{hao93, lu846, nmiguel, mous}@purdue.edu</span>.</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>The experimental video is available at <a href="https://youtu.be/iYwDWh-JNkU" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://youtu.be/iYwDWh-JNkU</a>.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper considers the problem of adapting a predesigned policy, represented by a parameterized function class, from a solution that minimizes a given original cost function to a trade-off solution between minimizing the original objective and an additional cost function. The problem is formulated as a constrained optimization problem, where deviations from the optimal value of the original cost are explicitly constrained. To solve it, we develop a closed-loop system that governs the evolution of the policy parameters, with a closed-loop controller designed to adjust the additional cost gradient to ensure the satisfaction of the constraint. The resulting closed-loop system, termed control–barrier–function–based policy adaptation, exploits the set-invariance property of control barrier functions to guarantee constraint satisfaction. The effectiveness of the proposed method is demonstrated through numerical experiments on the Cartpole and Lunar Lander benchmarks from OpenAI Gym, as well as a quadruped robot, thereby illustrating both its practicality and potential for real-world policy adaptation.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning (RL) has recently gained significant attention in robotics due to the growing complexity of robotic systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and the challenges posed by robotic environments. For instance, interactions in manipulation and locomotion tasks are difficult to predict, and the intricate dynamics of contact and friction forces are challenging to model accurately <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. To address these challenges, RL offers a model-free way for learning optimal policies that maximize cumulative rewards (or equivalently minimize cumulative stage costs) over time, without requiring explicit knowledge of the system dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In standard RL, robots learn policies through trial-and-error interactions with the environment, commonly implemented using an actor-critic framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, where the critic evaluates policy performance and the actor updates the policy parameters accordingly. However, training optimal policies from scratch in high-dimensional, nonlinear robotic systems often demands a large number of training episodes. Moreover, when task specifications change or new objectives are introduced, retraining from scratch may significantly increase both time and computational costs. These challenges highlight the importance of efficient policy adaptation methods that enable robots to achieve new objectives while leveraging existing pretrained policies.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/graphic_abstract_1_min.png" id="S1.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/190;" width="476" height="190" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pretrained policy: obstacle 1 avoidance (red trajectory). Adapted policy 1: obstacle 1 avoidance + goal reaching (blue trajectory).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/graphic_abstract_2_min.png" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/235;" width="476" height="235" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pretrained policy: equivalently adapted policy 1, obstacle 1 avoidance + goal reaching (blue trajectory). Adapted policy 2: obstacle 1 avoidance + goal reaching + additional obstacle 2 avoidance (green trajectory).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A robot executes pretrained policies to perform original tasks. Adapted policies enable the robot to fulfill both original tasks and additional tasks.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Control barrier functions (CBFs) were introduced in response to the growing need for feasible safety-critical controller design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Traditional safety-critical optimal control (OC) methods either fail to account for system dynamics feasibility or incorporate safety via potentially nonlinear constraints. These methods can lead to computational intractability and suffer from the curse of dimensionality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. To address these issues, CBFs provide safety guarantees while maintaining computational tractability, leveraging Nagumo’s principle of invariance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. The application of CBFs in existing research primarily focuses on collision avoidance, where the set of states leading to collisions is defined as the unsafe set, which is then treated as a constraint within a CBF quadratic programming (CBF-QP) problem. In those frameworks, the CBF-QP minimally adjusts the reference control input to avoid entering the unsafe set. Consequently, the CBF-QP is typically considered an additional control to a reference closed-loop controller, providing corrections to the reference input when safety risks are detected. While CBFs have become well-established in collision avoidance contexts, their use for optimality guarantees is less explored. Notably, recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> explores the use of CBFs to ensure optimality, utilizing them to design safe gradient flows for solving constrained nonlinear optimization problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the above challenges of policy adaptation and the promising capabilities of CBFs, this paper presents a closed-loop system that governs the dynamics of policy parameters. The proposed method minimizes an additional objective while ensuring near-optimal performance with respect to the original task throughout the learning process. The main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate policy adaptation in RL as a constrained optimization problem over the parameters of a predesigned optimal policy, where the additional objective is minimized subject to constraints on the deviation from the original optimal cost.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a closed-loop system for solving the constrained problem, in which a closed-loop controller modifies the gradient of the additional objective to ensure bounded deviation while improving efficiency by avoiding repeated solutions of the original optimization problem.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span> </span><span id="S1.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Related Work</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection provides an overview of related work on policy adaptation, focusing on the areas of
tuning OC systems and transfer learning in RL.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span>1 </span>Tuning OC Systems</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RL and OC both aim to design closed-loop controllers that minimize an objective function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Each relies on a system defined by states and inputs, with a model (plant or environment) describing state transitions. Tuning OC systems means adjusting parameters within the OC framework to minimize an additional loss function and meet extra performance requirements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Early work in the OC community approached this through neighboring extremal optimal control (NEOC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. NEOC studies how small variations in control preserve optimality when the initial condition of a nonlinear system changes slightly. This is achieved via second-variation theory, which reduces the problem to solving a time-varying linear-quadratic control problem along the original optimal trajectory.
Subsequent research examined perturbations in other factors, such as changes in parameters within the loss function. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> addressed time-varying parameters in nonlinear OC and proposed an algorithm to compute gradients of the optimal index with respect to these parameters, reusing pre-computed solutions. Later work, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, studied how larger parameter shifts affect nonlinear OC. Since most of these methods were continuous-time, extending NEOC to discrete-time systems became a natural step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, Pontryagin’s Differentiable Programming (PDP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> differentiates Pontryagin’s Maximum Principle (PMP), allowing parameters to appear in controls, objectives, and dynamics.
Building on PDP, a cooperative tuning method is proposed for multi-agent systems, where optimal trajectories are tuned in a distributed fashion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
Although NEOC and PMP-based approaches are effective, they require knowledge of the full optimal trajectory—a significant limitation in robotics, where tasks often have infinite horizons or high complexity. They also involve solving extra linear-quadratic problems, which become intractable in high-dimensional systems. To overcome these challenges, an online control-informed learning framework was recently introduced <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Unlike trajectory-based methods, it incrementally tunes OC systems using single data points in a recursive fashion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span>2 </span>Transfer Learning</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transfer Learning (TL), originally introduced in educational psychology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, is a machine learning paradigm where knowledge from one domain or task improves performance in another. In RL, TL accelerates learning in new but related tasks by leveraging prior experience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and knowledge can also be transferred through expert action distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Methodologically, TL is commonly categorized into reward shaping, inter-task mapping, representation transfer, and policy transfer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Reward shaping adjusts the reward distribution in the target domain using external knowledge to guide policy learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. A related line of work incrementally adapts reward functions based on human feedback, which can also be viewed as interactive reward shaping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Inter-task mapping focuses on mapping state–action spaces between source and target domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, while representation transfer leverages shared feature representations such as value functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> or Q-functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Of these, policy transfer is most relevant to this paper. Here, knowledge is encoded in pretrained source-task policies and transferred to the target task. Policy distillation is one approach, where multiple expert policies are distilled into a student policy by minimizing divergence in action distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Another is policy reuse, which directly incorporates source policies into target learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Policy transfer is closely related to imitation learning, as it discourages deviations from the source policy. However, the transferred policy may not always replicate source behavior during training or deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While TL methods primarily transfer knowledge from source tasks, this paper addresses policy adaptation in RL by leveraging the <span id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">invariant set</span> property of control barrier functions (CBFs), offering a conceptually distinct approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> formulates the problem of interest. Section <a href="#S3" title="III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> presents the proposed algorithm and its associated theoretical guarantees. Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> discusses the optimization strategy and the selection of key parameters, and further demonstrates the application of the method to policy adaptation in RL through simulation studies. Section <a href="#S5" title="V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> describes hardware experiments that validate the effectiveness of the approach. Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Concluding Remarks ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> concludes the paper and discusses limitations and future directions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notations.</span>
<math id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lVert\cdot\lVert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0.055em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\cdot\lVert</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Euclidean norm. Given a matrix <math id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes its transpose.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">The Problem</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section first formulates the problem under consideration and subsequently interprets it as a policy adaptation problem within the reinforcement learning (RL) framework.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Formulation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the vector of tunable parameters. Define <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}):\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}):\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta}):\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta}):\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as the original and additional objective functions, respectively, both assumed to be Lipschitz continuous. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a given minimizer of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">problem of interest</span> is to find the optimal parameters of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> within a bounded neighborhood near the known reference <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, this neighborhood is formalized as the following constraint:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a relaxation variable, which measures the tolerance for deviation from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is introduced to address cases where the optimal solution sets of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> do not intersect. By allowing a bounded relaxation with respect to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, the inclusion of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> ensures feasible solutions that balance performance across conflicting objectives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, this paper aims to solve the following constrained optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{(\boldsymbol{\theta},c)\in\mathbb{R}^{p+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{(\boldsymbol{\theta},c)\in\mathbb{R}^{p+1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(\boldsymbol{\theta})+wc^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(\boldsymbol{\theta})+wc^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E2Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{subject to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>subject to</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{subject to}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta})}_{h(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+c\geq 0,\ c\geq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta})}_{h(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+c\geq 0,\ c\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the constant penalty weight for <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*},c^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*},c^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the optimal solution of (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Problem (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) can be interpreted as a policy adaptation problem in RL. When <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, policy adaptation corresponds to finding <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that jointly minimizes both <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> while starting from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the solution <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a trade-off between minimizing <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, while allowing a maximum relaxation <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of the reference <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. The weight parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> governs this trade-off: for sufficiently small <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, the objective in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) emphasizes minimizing <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas for sufficiently large <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, the emphasis shifts toward minimizing <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>. Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A An Illustration Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> provides simulation results that empirically validate this behavior. The detailed connection between (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and policy adaptation in RL is discussed in the following subsection.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the context of policy adaptation in RL, the formulation in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) differs fundamentally from conventional unconstrained multi-objective optimization approaches, which are typically formulated as</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})+w\parallel G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta})\parallel^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})+w\parallel G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta})\parallel^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This objective incorporates a penalty term to discourage deviation from the reference solution <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝛉</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is often initialized via warm-start parameters <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝛉</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, the proposed problem in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) introduces a relaxation variable <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to treat the deviation from <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝛉</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> as a constraint, while minimizing a quadratic penalty on the maximum deviation, i.e., <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This formulation explicitly characterizes the trade-off between minimizing <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> through the tolerance variable <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be regulated using standard constrained optimization methods. As demonstrated in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, for the same penalty weight <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, the proposed formulation (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) yields smaller deviations from <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝛉</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> compared to (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Remark 1 ‣ II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a standard RL setup, where an agent interacts with an environment at discrete-time steps <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,1,2,\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,1,2,\cdots</annotation></semantics></math>. At any time <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, given a system state <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}(t)\in\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}(t)\in\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent selects the control input <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(t)\in\mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(t)\in\mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> according to a parameterized policy/actor:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}):\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}):\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a function with a known structure parameterized by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Upon executing the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent moves to a new state <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}(t+1)\sim\mathcal{P}(\cdot|\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{u}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}(t+1)\sim\mathcal{P}(\cdot|\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{u}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an unknown Markov transition kernel that describes the evolution of the system states. In the rest of this paper, we denote <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as constant state and input vectors, respectively, to distinguish them from the state and input variables.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>1 </span>Policy Adaptation Problem in RL</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Objectives of actor-critic algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> usually involve finding a policy that minimizes the discounted sum of stage costs, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})=\underset{\boldsymbol{x}(t)\sim\mathcal{P}_{0},\boldsymbol{u}(s)\sim\boldsymbol{\mu}}{\mathbb{E}}[\sum_{s=t}^{T}\hat{\gamma}^{s-t}\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s))]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒫</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝝁</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})=\underset{\boldsymbol{x}(t)\sim\mathcal{P}_{0},\boldsymbol{u}(s)\sim\boldsymbol{\mu}}{\mathbb{E}}[\sum_{s=t}^{T}\hat{\gamma}^{s-t}\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s))],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the distribution of the initial state <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is the control input generated from the policy in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\hat{\gamma}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\hat{\gamma}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is a discount factor, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s)):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s)):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stage cost function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since computing <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) requires knowledge of the unknown transition kernel <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, actor–critic methods approximate the optimal policy that minimizes <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> in a model-free manner. This is achieved by defining the action-value function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Q}_{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}_{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>, which quantifies the expected return starting from a fixed state–action pair <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and subsequently following policy <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). It is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{Q}_{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})=\underset{\boldsymbol{x}_{s&gt;t}\sim\mathcal{P},\boldsymbol{u}_{s&gt;t}\sim\boldsymbol{\mu}}{\mathbb{E}}[\sum_{s=t}^{T}\hat{\gamma}^{s-t}\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s))|\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>μ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">&gt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒖</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">&gt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝝁</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}_{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})=\underset{\boldsymbol{x}_{s&gt;t}\sim\mathcal{P},\boldsymbol{u}_{s&gt;t}\sim\boldsymbol{\mu}}{\mathbb{E}}[\sum_{s=t}^{T}\hat{\gamma}^{s-t}\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s))|\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then, an optimal critic function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})</annotation></semantics></math>, is used to approximate the action-value function, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{Q}(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*}):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*}):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined with known structure and constant parameters <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*}\in\mathbb{R}^{\hat{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*}\in\mathbb{R}^{\hat{q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})=\hat{Q}_{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>μ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})=\hat{Q}_{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, the optimal policy that minimizes (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is indirectly obtained by minimizing the critic evaluation:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the known stationary discounted state visitation distribution induced by policy <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given that the optimal policy for minimizing the original objective in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) has been obtained using actor–critic methods, i.e., the optimal actor <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is available. The policy adaptation problem in RL seeks to further tune <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a trade-off solution between minimizing the original objective in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and a secondary optimization problem defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}(t)\sim\mathcal{P}_{0},\boldsymbol{u}(s)\sim\boldsymbol{\mu}}[\sum_{s=t}^{T}\gamma^{s-t}\phi(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s))]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}(t)\sim\mathcal{P}_{0},\boldsymbol{u}(s)\sim\boldsymbol{\mu}}[\sum_{s=t}^{T}\gamma^{s-t}\phi(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s))],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\gamma\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\gamma\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is the discount factor, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s)):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\boldsymbol{x}(s),\boldsymbol{u}(s)):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the stage cost function, and control input <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) initialized at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>2 </span>Connection Between the Proposed Constrained Optimization and Policy Adaptation in RL</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The policy adaptation problem in RL can be cast as the constrained optimization in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) by setting <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}</annotation></semantics></math> as the parameters of the predesigned optimal policy, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> as the adaptable policy parameters, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> as the original objective in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and the additional objective in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), respectively. Specifically, this yields the optimization problem</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu},c)\in\mathbb{R}^{p+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu},c)\in\mathbb{R}^{p+1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})+wc^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})+wc^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{subject to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>subject to</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{subject to}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})+c\geq 0,\ c\geq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})+c\geq 0,\ c\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pretrained optimal policy parameters obtained from (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> is the original objective defined in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> as the secondary objective defined in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Main Results</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first highlight the key challenges and principles underlying the solution of (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Building on these insights, we introduce a closed-loop dynamical system to address (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and provide a theoretical analysis establishing constraint satisfaction throughout the optimization process.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Challenges and Key Ideas</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> have explored how solutions to constrained optimization problems can be interpreted as closed-loop control laws acting on the dynamics of the decision variables. This control-oriented perspective models optimization algorithms as closed-loop systems, enabling the application of stability, invariance, and robustness theory to algorithm design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by these developments, we consider the following continuous-time parameter dynamics to solve (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})+\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta},c^{*})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})+\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta},c^{*}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{c}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{c}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\psi(\boldsymbol{\theta})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\psi(\boldsymbol{\theta}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta},c^{*}):\mathbb{R}^{p}\times\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta},c^{*}):\mathbb{R}^{p}\times\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a closed-loop controller introduced to enforce the constraint in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(\boldsymbol{\theta}):\mathbb{R}^{p}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\boldsymbol{\theta}):\mathbb{R}^{p}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> characterizes the dynamics of the minimal relaxation constant <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, to be determined. We assume that the gradient <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz continuous. Since <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta},c^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta},c^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> depends on both <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> itself is determined by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt the shorthand notation <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta},c^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta},c^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> in the rest of this paper. For brevity, given any <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the constraint-admissible set associated with (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}=\{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}:h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*}\geq 0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}=\{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}:h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*}\geq 0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Three main challenges arise in the design of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">1) When <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is highly nonlinear, deriving a closed-form expression for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> that enforces the constraint (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is difficult.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">2) Since the constraint in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is defined directly in terms of the parameter <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is necessary to construct a feasible set for the parameter dynamics <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\dot{\theta},c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\dot{\theta},c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that if <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized at <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}\in\mathcal{C}_{\dot{\theta},c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}\in\mathcal{C}_{\dot{\theta},c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">3) Efficiently determining the dynamics corresponding to the minimal relaxation constant (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) is nontrivial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we construct the feasible set <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\dot{\theta},c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\dot{\theta},c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the dynamics in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) based on the set-invariance property of continuous-time control barrier functions (CBFs), which enables the nonlinear constraint (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to be reformulated as a linear constraint without any relaxation. Based on this formulation, we design a quadratic programming (QP) that simultaneously determines <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feasible set construction for parameter update direction.</span> Given the parameter dynamics in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), we have the following definitions related to CBFs along with their fundamental property:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (CBFs for Continuous-time Dynamical Systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A continuously differentiable function <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\boldsymbol{\theta}):\mathbb{R}^{p}\rightarrow\mathbb{R},B\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\boldsymbol{\theta}):\mathbb{R}^{p}\rightarrow\mathbb{R},B\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a CBF if there exists an extended class <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> function <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝛉</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, it meets the following condition:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\boldsymbol{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}\{L_{f}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})+L_{g}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})\boldsymbol{a}\}\geq-\kappa(B(\boldsymbol{\theta}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\boldsymbol{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}\{L_{f}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})+L_{g}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})\boldsymbol{a}\}\geq-\kappa(B(\boldsymbol{\theta})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})=-\frac{\partial B(\boldsymbol{\theta})}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})=-\frac{\partial B(\boldsymbol{\theta})}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\frac{\partial B(\boldsymbol{\theta})}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\frac{\partial B(\boldsymbol{\theta})}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times p}</annotation></semantics></math> are Lie derivatives.
Accordingly, we define the superlevel set of <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> as the safe set <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}:B(\boldsymbol{\theta})\geq 0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}:B(\boldsymbol{\theta})\geq 0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Constraint Satisfaction with CBFs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose the dynamical system is defined as in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), with a CBF <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> specified in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (CBFs for Continuous-time Dynamical Systems []) ‣ III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and the corresponding safe set <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> given in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). Any Lipschitz continuous controller that satisfies (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Definition 1 (CBFs for Continuous-time Dynamical Systems []) ‣ III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) ensures that the safe set <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> remains invariant, thus guaranteeing constraint satisfaction of <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\boldsymbol{\theta})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\boldsymbol{\theta})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">According to Definitions <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (CBFs for Continuous-time Dynamical Systems []) ‣ III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Constraint Satisfaction with CBFs []) ‣ III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we define the feasible set <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}},c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}},c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the CBFs framework by treating <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as a candidate CBF. The validity of this candidate will be formally established in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Analysis ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-C</span></a>. By Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Constraint Satisfaction with CBFs []) ‣ III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, any <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies the CBF inequality guarantees forward invariance of the set <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Consequently, for any <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we introduce the following CBF inequality constraint:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{L_{f}+L_{g}\boldsymbol{a}+\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})}_{\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{a},c^{*})}\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{L_{f}+L_{g}\boldsymbol{a}+\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})}_{\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{a},c^{*})}\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E15X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E15Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}=-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}=-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.4" class="ltx_p">are Lie derivatives of the function <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> under the parameter dynamics given in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Furthermore, by applying (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{a},c^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{a},c^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) can be equivalently written as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{a},c^{*})=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}+\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{a},c^{*})=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}+\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.5" class="ltx_p">which leads to the definition of the following feasible set of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}},c^{*}}=\{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}:\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}+\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})\geq 0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}},c^{*}}=\{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}:\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{\prime}\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}+\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})\geq 0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As shown in (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), finding the control input <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) is equivalent to determining the parameters update direction <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}\in\mathcal{C}_{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}},c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}\in\mathcal{C}_{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}},c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, according to the properties of CBFs stated in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Constraint Satisfaction with CBFs []) ‣ III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, if the parameters <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized within the set <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then any update direction <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}\in\mathcal{C}_{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}},c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}\in\mathcal{C}_{\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}},c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> guarantees that the corresponding parameter trajectories remains within <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> throughout the evolution.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construct the QP problem.</span> Consider the parameter dynamics in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and the feasible set specified in (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>). We construct the following QP problem to derive <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E17X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}),c^{*})=\arg\min_{(\boldsymbol{a},c)\in\mathbb{R}^{p+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}),c^{*})=\arg\min_{(\boldsymbol{a},c)\in\mathbb{R}^{p+1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\parallel\boldsymbol{a}\parallel^{2}+\frac{w}{2}c^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>c</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\parallel\boldsymbol{a}\parallel^{2}+\frac{w}{2}c^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E17Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{subject to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>subject to</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{subject to}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{a},c)\geq 0,\ c\geq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{a},c)\geq 0,\ c\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) is formulated to derive the closed-loop controller <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and the minimal relaxation constant <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that enforce the CBF inequality in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), thereby guaranteeing satisfaction of constraint (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), while simultaneously minimizing both quantities.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Proposed Closed-Loop System</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the parameter dynamics in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), where the parameter is initialized as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. We introduce the proposed closed-loop controller <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> for (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) to solve (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which corresponds to the closed-form solution of (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), as formalized in the following lemma. In the remainder of this paper, we refer to dynamics (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) with proposed <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">control-barrier-function-based policy adaptation</span> (CBF-PA) algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c)=\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c)=\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">h</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> a given constant, then the closed-loop controller <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and minimal relaxation constant <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that solve (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) are:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}),c^{*})=\begin{cases}(\mathbf{0}_{p},0),&amp;\text{if}\ L_{a}\geq 0,\\
(\frac{-L_{a}L_{g}^{\prime}}{L_{g}L_{g}^{\prime}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}^{2}/w},L_{b}),&amp;\text{if}\ L_{a}&lt;0,L_{b}\geq 0,\\
(\frac{-L_{a}L_{g}^{\prime}}{L_{g}L_{g}^{\prime}},0),&amp;\text{if}\ L_{a}&lt;0,L_{b}&lt;0,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if</mtext><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if</mtext><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}),c^{*})=\begin{cases}(\mathbf{0}_{p},0),&amp;\text{if}\ L_{a}\geq 0,\\
(\frac{-L_{a}L_{g}^{\prime}}{L_{g}L_{g}^{\prime}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}^{2}/w},L_{b}),&amp;\text{if}\ L_{a}&lt;0,L_{b}\geq 0,\\
(\frac{-L_{a}L_{g}^{\prime}}{L_{g}L_{g}^{\prime}},0),&amp;\text{if}\ L_{a}&lt;0,L_{b}&lt;0,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the auxiliary terms are given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq L_{f}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}(G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq L_{f}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}(G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex5Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{b}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq-\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}L_{a}/(wL_{g}L_{g}^{\prime}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}^{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">≔</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq-\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}L_{a}/(wL_{g}L_{g}^{\prime}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the special case where <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a given constant. Under the same assumptions and notations as in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the following closed-form solution holds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\begin{cases}\mathbf{0}_{p},&amp;\text{if}\ L_{a}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}c\geq 0,\\
\frac{-(L_{a}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}c)L_{g}^{\prime}}{L_{g}L_{g}^{\prime}},&amp;\text{else}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">else</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\begin{cases}\mathbf{0}_{p},&amp;\text{if}\ L_{a}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}c\geq 0,\\
\frac{-(L_{a}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}c)L_{g}^{\prime}}{L_{g}L_{g}^{\prime}},&amp;\text{else}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The proof proceeds analogously to the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and is thus omitted for brevity.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection presents a theoretical analysis of the proposed closed-loop controller in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for solving the constrained optimization problem in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Since this paper utilizes the properties of CBFs to ensure (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is satisfied, it is essential to verify that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall c\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall c\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the function <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is a valid CBF for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c}</annotation></semantics></math>. To this end, we establish the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the parameter dynamics in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), for any <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is a valid CBF for <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 ‣ III-C Analysis ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is given in the Appendix.
The following theorem establishes the constraint satisfaction associated with the optimal closed-loop controller in (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the parameter dynamics (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>). Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized as <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝛉</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝛉</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) is locally Lipschitz continuous and bounded, and the class-<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> function <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})=\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})=\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}(h(\boldsymbol{\theta})+c^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">h</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, if <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves according to (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), it follows that <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathcal{C}_{\theta,c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 ‣ III-C Analysis ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Applying the Proposed CBF-PA to Policy Adaptation in RL</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now propose an algorithm that integrates the CBF-PA mechanism to address the policy adaptation problem in RL. The approach extends the deep deterministic policy gradient (DDPG) method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> by employing CBF-PA as the policy update rule. In contrast to standard DDPG, the proposed CBF-PA algorithm refines the learned policy to minimize an additional task objective while preserving near-optimal performance on the original task. This refinement is achieved by augmenting the policy gradient with the analytically derived closed-loop controller in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The design builds upon the DDPG framework, where the optimal critic <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q*})</annotation></semantics></math> is used to approximate the action-value function of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). The optimal policy parameters <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu*}</annotation></semantics></math> is then obtained by minimizing the critic feedback, i.e., <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q*})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q*})]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the discounted state visitation distribution under policy <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To initialize the proposed algorithm, it is assumed that a pretrained optimal critic <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{Q}(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})</annotation></semantics></math>, as defined in (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), and an optimal policy <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})</annotation></semantics></math>, as specified in (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), are already available. The learning process begins by constructing a new critic function <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q}):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q}):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and initializing a policy <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> share the same functional structure, with the initial policy parameters set as <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}=\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}=\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon the actor-critic framework, we propose an iterative method for estimating the optimal parameters <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,2,\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,2,\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> denote the iteration index, and let <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the estimates of <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu*}</annotation></semantics></math> at the <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration, respectively. The constant step sizes for updating the critic and actor parameters are denoted by <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mu}=\epsilon\alpha_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mu}=\epsilon\alpha_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, where <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant used to ensure that the critic update proceeds at a faster rate than the actor update. The following iterative update rules are employed to refine these estimates:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span>1 </span>Critic Update</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To find the optimal critic that approximates the action-value function of the objective function in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B1 Policy Adaptation Problem in RL ‣ II-B Problem Interpretation as Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), the critic parameters <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{Q}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}-\alpha_{Q}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q}}{\mathbf{L}}_{Q}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\quad\boldsymbol{\theta}_{0}^{Q},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{0}^{\mu}\ \text{given}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup></msub><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>given</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{Q}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}-\alpha_{Q}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q}}{\mathbf{L}}_{Q}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\quad\boldsymbol{\theta}_{0}^{Q},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{0}^{\mu}\ \text{given},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{L}}_{Q}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{L}}_{Q}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the temporal difference learning loss function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> defined by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbf{L}}_{Q}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu},\boldsymbol{u}_{t}\sim\boldsymbol{\mu}}[(Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q})-\phi(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐋</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbf{L}}_{Q}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu},\boldsymbol{u}_{t}\sim\boldsymbol{\mu}}[(Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q})-\phi(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex7Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}))^{2}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}))^{2}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span>2 </span>Policy Adaptation using CBF-PA</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">After updating <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-D1 Critic Update ‣ III-D Applying the Proposed CBF-PA to Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), the policy parameters <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is subsequently updated using the proposed CBF-PA, given by:</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E20X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{\mu}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}-\alpha_{\mu}\Big(\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{Q})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{\mu}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}-\alpha_{\mu}\Big(\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{Q})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E20Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) with the components:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex9X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cdot\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{Q})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cdot\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{Q})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})^{\prime}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})^{\prime}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9Xc"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=L_{f}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=L_{f}+\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9Xd"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9Xd.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The complete procedure is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-D2 Policy Adaptation using CBF-PA ‣ III-D Applying the Proposed CBF-PA to Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Control-Barrier-Function-Based Policy Adaptation (CBF-PA)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="alg1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize:</span> Pretrained optimal policy <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})</annotation></semantics></math> and optimal critic <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{Q}(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}*})</annotation></semantics></math>; Empty data memory; initialize <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\cdot,\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}=\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}=\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the iteration index <math id="alg1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>; copy <math id="alg1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Q}\leftarrow Q,\ \boldsymbol{\bar{\mu}}\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Q}\leftarrow Q,\ \boldsymbol{\bar{\mu}}\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
              Set the number of episodes <math id="alg1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, task
horizon <math id="alg1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, update weight <math id="alg1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and step sizes <math id="alg1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="alg1.4.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="alg1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{episode}=0,1,2,\cdots,E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{episode}=0,1,2,\cdots,E</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="alg1.4.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
              Initialize random process <math id="alg1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> for action exploration and randomize initial state <math id="alg1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="alg1.4.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="alg1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,1,2,\cdots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,1,2,\cdots,T</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="alg1.4.5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
              Execute control input <math id="alg1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+W(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+W(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, observe resulting <math id="alg1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and store data tuple <math id="alg1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{u}_{t},\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> in data memory
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
              Sample <math id="alg1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> tuples uniformly from data memory
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
              Update critic and actor following (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-D1 Critic Update ‣ III-D Applying the Proposed CBF-PA to Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-D2 Policy Adaptation using CBF-PA ‣ III-D Applying the Proposed CBF-PA to Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>), respectively, wherein the expectation terms are approximated using the averaged values computed over the <math id="alg1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampled tuples
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
               <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
              Update the copied networks <math id="alg1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>:

<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{Q}}&amp;\leftarrow\tau\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q}+(1-\tau)\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{Q}},\\
\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{\mu}}&amp;\leftarrow\tau\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}+(1-\tau)\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{\mu}}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{Q}}&amp;\leftarrow\tau\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q}+(1-\tau)\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{Q}},\\
\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{\mu}}&amp;\leftarrow\tau\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}+(1-\tau)\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{\mu}}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return: </span> <math id="alg1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{Q}},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{\mu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{Q}},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\bar{\mu}}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-E</span> </span><span id="S3.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Discussion of Discrete-Time Implementation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\alpha_{\mu}\rightarrow 0}\frac{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{\mu}-\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}}{\alpha_{\mu}}=\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}^{\mu}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\alpha_{\mu}\rightarrow 0}\frac{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}^{\mu}-\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu}}{\alpha_{\mu}}=\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}^{\mu},</annotation></semantics></math> the policy update rule in (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-D2 Policy Adaptation using CBF-PA ‣ III-D Applying the Proposed CBF-PA to Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) can be interpreted, as <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mu}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mu}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, as the Euler discretization of the continuous-time dynamics</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}^{\mu}=-\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q})]+\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\dot{\theta}}^{\mu}=-\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\sim\rho_{\mu}}[\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}}Q(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{Q})]+\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This scheme introduces a discretization error of order <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\alpha_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\alpha_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> per gradient descent step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, thereby causing a modeling discrepancy due to the nonzero <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure that the discrete-time implementation in (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-D2 Policy Adaptation using CBF-PA ‣ III-D Applying the Proposed CBF-PA to Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) using <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the performance guarantees of the continuous-time formulation in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) with <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), we adopt the following result:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlemma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Inter-Sample Safety Guarantees, Theorem <math id="Thmlemma3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall the policy parameter dynamics from (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and the CBF <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (CBFs for Continuous-time Dynamical Systems []) ‣ III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Suppose that <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝛉</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}\in\mathcal{A}\subset\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}\in\mathcal{A}\subset\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded. Let <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\geq\frac{\alpha}{2}L_{B}\sup_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p},\boldsymbol{a}\in\mathcal{A}}\lVert-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})+\boldsymbol{a}\rVert," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\geq\frac{\alpha}{2}L_{B}\sup_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{p},\boldsymbol{a}\in\mathcal{A}}\lVert-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})+\boldsymbol{a}\rVert,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Lipschitz constant of <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, it holds that the closed-loop controller <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following tightened CBF inequality constraint of (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>):</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})+L_{g}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})\boldsymbol{a}+\kappa(B(\boldsymbol{\theta})-\Delta)\geq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})+L_{g}B(\boldsymbol{\theta})\boldsymbol{a}+\kappa(B(\boldsymbol{\theta})-\Delta)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">will guarantee the invariance of the <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-A Challenges and Key Ideas ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3 (Inter-Sample Safety Guarantees, Theorem 3 []) ‣ III-E Discussion of Discrete-Time Implementation ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> says that if the discrepancy between the continuous-time dynamics and its Euler-discretized counterpart is bounded, then the control performance guaranteed by the CBF condition in the continuous-time setting can be preserved in the discretized system by employing a more conservative CBF inequality constraint.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Numerical Simulations</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, the proposed CBF-PA is first applied to a simple illustrative example, facilitating a direct comparison with standard gradient descent and the multi-objective optimization in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Remark 1 ‣ II-A Problem Formulation ‣ II The Problem ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). This example also provides insight into the impact of key parameters, including the penalty weight <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, step size <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and CBF parameter <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, on optimization performance. Subsequently, Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-D2 Policy Adaptation using CBF-PA ‣ III-D Applying the Proposed CBF-PA to Policy Adaptation in RL ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is evaluated in two benchmark simulation environments, Cartpole and lunar lander, from OpenAI Gym <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, to assess its effectiveness and generalizability. For additional comparison, the method is evaluated against two representative transfer learning approaches, thereby highlighting its relative advantages.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An Illustration Example</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}=[x,y]^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}=[x,y]^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the additional and original objective functions as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\sin(x)+(y-8)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\sin(x)+(y-8)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta})=x^{3}+y^{3},\text{s.t.,}\ x\geq 0,y\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t.,</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta})=x^{3}+y^{3},\text{s.t.,}\ x\geq 0,y\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The goal is to solve the optimization problem <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}}J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, starting from a known initialization <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}=\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}^{\top}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta}),\ \text{s.t.}\ x\geq 0,y\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}=\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}^{\top}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}}G(\boldsymbol{\theta}),\ \text{s.t.}\ x\geq 0,y\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that for any <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}=[-x,x]^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}=[-x,x]^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G([-x,x]^{\prime})=G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G([-x,x]^{\prime})=G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This setup is designed to evaluate the algorithm’s ability to preserve the performance on the pre-optimized task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> while minimizing <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. Accordingly, the performance metrics are defined as additional cost function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, original cost function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, and average value of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> iterations:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{G}=\textstyle\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{G}=\textstyle\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}G(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span> This experiment examines how different constant weight parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> influence the performance metric of the discrete-time implementation of the proposed method, defined as</p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E21X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}-\alpha(\nabla_{\theta}J(\boldsymbol{\theta_{k}})-\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>𝒌</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}-\alpha(\nabla_{\theta}J(\boldsymbol{\theta_{k}})-\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is given in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ III-B The Proposed Closed-Loop System ‣ III Main Results ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We compare (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In IV-A An Illustration Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) with two baseline methods: standard gradient descent (GD) and a multi-objective gradient descent (MOGD). The GD method follows the classical update rule:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}-\alpha\nabla_{\theta}J(\boldsymbol{\theta_{k}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>𝒌</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}-\alpha\nabla_{\theta}J(\boldsymbol{\theta_{k}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">while the MOGD baseline incorporates a regularization term involving <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}-\alpha\nabla_{\theta}(J(\boldsymbol{\theta_{k}})+w(G(\boldsymbol{\theta_{k}})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}))^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>𝒌</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>𝒌</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k+1}=\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}-\alpha\nabla_{\theta}(J(\boldsymbol{\theta_{k}})+w(G(\boldsymbol{\theta_{k}})-G(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}))^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">For the proposed method, we set <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, and use a fixed step size <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.001</annotation></semantics></math> and initial parameter <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{G}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for all methods. By varying <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, we evaluate the relative optimization behavior and the trade-offs between minimizing the primary loss <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and maintaining performance with respect to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results Analysis.</span>
As illustrated in Figs. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A An Illustration Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-<a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A An Illustration Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the proposed method exhibits an optimization behavior similar to that of the MOGD algorithm under various weight parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, decreasing <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> drives the parameter trajectories of both methods toward the optimizer of the additional cost <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, as <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> increases, both methods yield parameter trajectories that approach the joint optimum of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, for larger values of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, the proposed CBF-PA demonstrates a faster convergence rate than MOGD.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/cbfpa_w.svg" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/364;" width="476" height="364"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Parameter trajectories.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/cbfpa_w_c.svg" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/339;" width="476" height="339"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Evolution of <math id="S4.F2.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of CBF-PA.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
