</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MOGD under various values of <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, shown over the contour plot of the function <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key Parameters Investigation.</span> To evaluate the impact of key parameters in the proposed method (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In IV-A An Illustration Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>), two sets of experiments are conducted. In the first set, the CBF parameter <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> is varied while fixing the step size <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.001</annotation></semantics></math> and penalty weight <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>. In the second set, the step size <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is varied with <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results Analysis.</span>
As shown in Figs. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A An Illustration Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>-<a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-A An Illustration Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, reducing <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> accelerates the convergence of both <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, when <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}\geq 50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{h}}\geq 50</annotation></semantics></math>, CBF-PA tends to prioritize minimizing the additional cost <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> over the original cost <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, reducing the step size <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> leads to slower convergence while yielding parameter trajectories similar to those of the proposed method with larger <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
