<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cartpole Example</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The state of the Cartpole system is represented as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}(t)=[p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\theta(t),\dot{\theta}(t)]^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}(t)=[p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\theta(t),\dot{\theta}(t)]^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-4.8\leq p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)\leq 4.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4.8</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>4.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-4.8\leq p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)\leq 4.8</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cart’s position, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cart’s velocity, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.418\leq\theta(t)\leq 0.418" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.418</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.418</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.418\leq\theta(t)\leq 0.418</annotation></semantics></math> is the pole’s angle, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\theta}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\theta}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the pole’s angular velocity. The control input <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1\leq\boldsymbol{u}(t)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1\leq\boldsymbol{u}(t)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is applied to the center of the cart to push the cart to move left or right. The dynamics of the Cartpole are given by:</p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\boldsymbol{x}(t+1)=\boldsymbol{x}(t)+\dot{\boldsymbol{x}}(t)\Delta t,\\
&amp;\ddot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}=\frac{u+lm_{\mathrm{p}}\dot{\theta}^{2}\sin(\theta)}{m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{p}}}-\frac{lm_{\mathrm{p}}\ddot{\theta}\cos(\theta)}{m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{p}}},\\
&amp;\ddot{\theta}=\frac{g\sin(\theta)-\cos(\theta)(u+lm_{\mathrm{p}}\dot{\theta}^{2}\sin(\theta))/(m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{p}})}{l(4/3-m_{\mathrm{p}}\cos(\theta)^{2}/(m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{p}}))},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\boldsymbol{x}(t+1)=\boldsymbol{x}(t)+\dot{\boldsymbol{x}}(t)\Delta t,\\
&amp;\ddot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}=\frac{u+lm_{\mathrm{p}}\dot{\theta}^{2}\sin(\theta)}{m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{p}}}-\frac{lm_{\mathrm{p}}\ddot{\theta}\cos(\theta)}{m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{p}}},\\
&amp;\ddot{\theta}=\frac{g\sin(\theta)-\cos(\theta)(u+lm_{\mathrm{p}}\dot{\theta}^{2}\sin(\theta))/(m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{p}})}{l(4/3-m_{\mathrm{p}}\cos(\theta)^{2}/(m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{p}}))},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the length of the pole, gravity, the mass of the cart, and the mass of the pole, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We set the time interval <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> seconds for simulating the dynamics in (<a href="#S4.E22" title="In IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) to generate the training data. The control inputs are derived from the policy <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})+W(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})+W(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(t)</annotation></semantics></math> represents exploration noise sampled from a normal distribution. The primary control objective for the Cartpole is to maintain the pole upright by appropriately moving the cart. For this, a predefined stage cost is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}(\theta(t))=\begin{cases}-1,\quad&amp;\text{if}-0.2095\leq\theta(t)\leq 0.2095,\\
0,\quad&amp;\text{else}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2095</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.2095</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>else</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}(\theta(t))=\begin{cases}-1,\quad&amp;\text{if}-0.2095\leq\theta(t)\leq 0.2095,\\
0,\quad&amp;\text{else}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The predesigned optimal policy <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and critic <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> are achieved by running the existing DDPG algorithm. An additional objective is to drive the cart to the position <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)=2</annotation></semantics></math>, for which we define the stage cost function as:</p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))=0.1(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-2)^{2}+0.001\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.001</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))=0.1(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-2)^{2}+0.001\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For comparison purposes, we define the stage cost for multi-objective RL as: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}^{MORL}=\hat{\phi}(\theta(t))+w\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{MORL}=\hat{\phi}(\theta(t))+w\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the stage cost based on the concept of behavior cloning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> from transfer learning as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi_{t}^{BC}=\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))+w(\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})-\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}))^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{BC}=\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))+w(\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})-\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*}))^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Finally, all algorithms are trained for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> episodes. Each episode terminates either when the state constraint is violated or when the time step <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math>. The initial state for each episode is randomly sampled from a uniform distribution within the range <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-0.05,-0.05,-0.05,-0.05]^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-0.05,-0.05,-0.05,-0.05]^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.05,0.05,0.05,0.05]^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.05,0.05,0.05,0.05]^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proposed method is deployed to minimize the accumulated cost <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E24" title="In IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>), while the DDPG algorithm is implemented to minimize <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}^{BC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{BC}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}^{MORL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{MORL}</annotation></semantics></math>. All methods are initialized from the same predesigned policy <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and critic <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. To mitigate the effects of stochasticity arising from random initialization and training of DNNs, all experiments are repeated over <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> independent trials.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/cartploe_train_ori_cost.svg" id="S4.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/324;" width="476" height="324"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of original task <math id="S4.F6.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}(\theta(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}(\theta(t))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/cartploe_train_add_cost.svg" id="S4.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/344;" width="476" height="344"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of additional task <math id="S4.F6.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training cost for the Cartpole example, where Fig. <a href="#S4.F6.sf1" title="In Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a> denotes the episode cost refers to the cumulative original stage cost <math id="S4.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) accumulated over each episode, while Fig. <a href="#S4.F6.sf2" title="In Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a> is the averaged episode cost represents the average additional stage cost <math id="S4.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E24" title="In IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>), accounting for variations in initial positions. The solid line in the plots illustrates the mean value across <math id="S4.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> experimental trials, and the shaded region indicates the standard deviation over these trials.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results Analysis.</span> Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates the training cost for all comparison algorithms. The proposed algorithm effectively preserves the original task, reaching a cost of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-499" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>499</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-499</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> training epochs across <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> trials. Moreover, it exhibits a faster convergence rate in minimizing the additional stage cost compared to the baseline methods. Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> illustrates the performance of adapted policies across all evaluated algorithms over <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> test episodes.
In Fig. <a href="#S4.F7.sf1" title="In Fig. 7 ‣ IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a>, all methods maintain optimal performance on the original task, successfully balancing the Cartpole for the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math> time steps for each episode, indicating that no method compromises the original objective.
In contrast, Fig. <a href="#S4.F7.sf2" title="In Fig. 7 ‣ IV-B Cartpole Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a> highlights clear differences in performance on the additional task: the proposed algorithm significantly reduces the additional stage cost compared to the baseline methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate statistical significance, a one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Chapter 14]</cite> is conducted. The analysis reveals a statistically significant difference in the additional task cost across methods (p-value <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> 0.05). Post-hoc comparisons using Tukey’s HSD test indicate the following relationship: Proposed algorithm <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> Behavior Cloning <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math> Multi-objective RL <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math> Pretrained model. This result confirms that the proposed algorithm achieves the additional objective more effectively without sacrificing performance on the original task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/cartpole_original.png" id="S4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of original task.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/cartpole_additional.png" id="S4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of additional task <math id="S4.F7.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of original and additional task for 50 test episodes with the Cartpole.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lunar Lander Example</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we evaluate the proposed CBF‑PA algorithm on the OpenAI Gym Lunar Lander environment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. The system state <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{8}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the horizontal and vertical positions, velocities, angular orientation, angular velocity, and ground-contact indicators of the lander and is subject to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1.5,-1.5,-5.0,-5.0,-3.14,-5.0,0,0]^{\prime}\leq\boldsymbol{x}(t)\leq[1.5,1.5,5.0,5.0,3.14,5.0,1.0,1.0]^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.14</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1.5,-1.5,-5.0,-5.0,-3.14,-5.0,0,0]^{\prime}\leq\boldsymbol{x}(t)\leq[1.5,1.5,5.0,5.0,3.14,5.0,1.0,1.0]^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The control input <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which commands the main and lateral thrusters, is similarly bounded by <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,-1]^{\prime}\leq\boldsymbol{u}(t)\leq[1,1]^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,-1]^{\prime}\leq\boldsymbol{u}(t)\leq[1,1]^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a detailed description of the underlying dynamics, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> As in the Cartpole example, the system state-input data pairs are generated using the policy <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})+W(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k}^{\mu})+W(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(t)</annotation></semantics></math> represents exploration noise drawn from a normal distribution. Each episode begins with the lunar lander spawned at a fixed initial pose, subject to a random perturbation at its center of mass, and terminates when either a state constraint is violated or the time index <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math>. The primary control objective is to achieve a soft touchdown at the target pad located at <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)=(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)=(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. The underlying predefined stage cost <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{u}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{u}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> are adopted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. Both the predesigned policy <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and critic <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained by running the DDPG algorithm. To evaluate the proposed CBF‑PA framework, an additional task to minimize energy consumption during the landing phase is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))=\boldsymbol{u}(t)^{\prime}\boldsymbol{u}(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))=\boldsymbol{u}(t)^{\prime}\boldsymbol{u}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For comparative analysis, we define the stage cost for multi-objective RL as: <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}^{MORL}=\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{u}(t))+w\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{MORL}=\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{u}(t))+w\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, and for behavior cloning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> as <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi_{t}^{BC}=\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))+w\|\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})-\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{BC}=\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))+w\|\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})-\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}*})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each algorithm was trained for <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> episodes and evaluated across <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> independent trials.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/lander_train_ori_cost.svg" id="S4.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/324;" width="476" height="324"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F8.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of original task.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/lander_train_add_cost.svg" id="S4.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/342;" width="476" height="342"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F8.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of additional task <math id="S4.F8.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 8</span>: </span><span id="S4.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training plots for lunar lander example, where Fig. <a href="#S4.F8.sf1" title="In Fig. 8 ‣ IV-C Lunar Lander Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(a)</span></a> denotes the episode rewards, which refers to the original rewards accumulated over each episode, while Fig. <a href="#S4.F8.sf2" title="In Fig. 8 ‣ IV-C Lunar Lander Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(b)</span></a> denotes the averaged episode cost, representing the average additional stage cost <math id="S4.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E25" title="In IV-C Lunar Lander Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>), accounting for variations in initial positions. The solid line in the plots illustrates the mean value across <math id="S4.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> experimental trials, and the shaded region indicates the standard deviation over these trials.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results Analysis.</span> Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ IV-C Lunar Lander Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> showcases the training process for all comparison algorithms. The proposed algorithm successfully maintains the original task over <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">400</annotation></semantics></math> training episodes across <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> trials. Moreover, it demonstrates faster convergence, reducing the additional stage cost within <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> episodes, surpassing the baseline methods in efficiency. Fig. <a href="#S4.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ IV-C Lunar Lander Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> presents the performance of the adapted policies across all evaluated algorithms over <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> test episodes.
In Fig. <a href="#S4.F9.sf1" title="In Fig. 9 ‣ IV-C Lunar Lander Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(a)</span></a>, all methods successfully maintain the objective on the original task, enabling the lunar lander to reach the designated landing position.
To assess statistical differences, an ANOVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Chapter 14]</cite> was conducted, which finds no statistically significant differences in the original task cost among methods (p-value <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, Fig. <a href="#S4.F9.sf2" title="In Fig. 9 ‣ IV-C Lunar Lander Example ‣ IV Numerical Simulations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(b)</span></a> reveals notable differences in the additional task performance. The proposed algorithm achieves a substantially lower additional stage cost, effectively minimizing energy consumption during the landing process compared to baseline methods.
An ANOVA confirms that the differences in additional task cost are statistically significant (p-value <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>). Post-hoc analysis using Tukey’s HSD test further identifies the following relationship: Proposed algorithm <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> Behavior Cloning <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math> Multi-objective RL <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math> Pretrained model.
These results demonstrate that the proposed algorithm enhances performance on the additional objective with an acceptable degradation in performance on the original task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/lunar_original.png" id="S4.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F9.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F9.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of original task.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F9.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/lunar_additional.png" id="S4.F9.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F9.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F9.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of additional task <math id="S4.F9.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\boldsymbol{u}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 9</span>: </span><span id="S4.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost of original and additional tasks for 50 test episodes with the lunar lander.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Real-World Demonstrations</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we demonstrate the performance of the proposed CBF-PA algorithm on a quadrupedal robot platform, highlighting its effectiveness in path planning adaptation scenarios. The kinematic dynamics of the quadrupedal robot are modeled as a unicycle model, described as:</p>
<table id="S5.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\dot{x}}=\begin{bmatrix}\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}\\
\dot{p}_{\mathrm{y}}\\
\dot{\theta}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}u_{\mathrm{v}}\cos(\theta)\\
u_{\mathrm{v}}\sin(\theta)\\
u_{\omega}\end{bmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\dot{x}}=\begin{bmatrix}\dot{p}_{\mathrm{x}}\\
\dot{p}_{\mathrm{y}}\\
\dot{\theta}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}u_{\mathrm{v}}\cos(\theta)\\
u_{\mathrm{v}}\sin(\theta)\\
u_{\omega}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the system state and control input at any time <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}(t)=\begin{bmatrix}p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),p_{\mathrm{y}}(t),\theta(t)\end{bmatrix}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}(t)=\begin{bmatrix}p_{\mathrm{x}}(t),p_{\mathrm{y}}(t),\theta(t)\end{bmatrix}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\begin{bmatrix}u_{\mathrm{v}}(t),u_{\omega}(t)\end{bmatrix}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}(t)=\begin{bmatrix}u_{\mathrm{v}}(t),u_{\omega}(t)\end{bmatrix}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Here, <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2.4\text{m}\leq p_{\mathrm{x}}\leq 2.4\text{m},-1.8\text{m}\leq p_{\mathrm{y}}\leq 1.6\text{m},-\pi\leq\theta\leq\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1.8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2.4\text{m}\leq p_{\mathrm{x}}\leq 2.4\text{m},-1.8\text{m}\leq p_{\mathrm{y}}\leq 1.6\text{m},-\pi\leq\theta\leq\pi</annotation></semantics></math> are the positions on the x-axis and y-axis, and yaw angle, respectively.
<math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1\text{ m/s}\leq u_{\mathrm{v}}\leq 1\text{ m/s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> m/s</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> m/s</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1\text{ m/s}\leq u_{\mathrm{v}}\leq 1\text{ m/s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-3\text{ rad/s}\leq u_{\omega}\leq 3\text{ rad/s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> rad/s</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> rad/s</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-3\text{ rad/s}\leq u_{\omega}\leq 3\text{ rad/s}</annotation></semantics></math> are the linear velocity and yaw angular velocity of the robot, respectively. The initial state is uniformly generated between <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1.9,-0.2,-0.2]^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1.9,-0.2,-0.2]^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1.5,0.2,0.2]^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1.5,0.2,0.2]^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. An illustration of this unicycle model is shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.02720v1/dog_intro.png" id="S5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/138;" width="143" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 10</span>: </span><span id="S5.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">High-level quadrupedal robot kinematics definition.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we apply the proposed method to adapt a pretrained policy that enables the robot to avoid obstacles while reaching a specified target position.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F11.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/before_traj_def_blur_min.png" id="S5.F11.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/98;" width="153" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F11.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F11.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/middle_90_traj_def_blur_min.png" id="S5.F11.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/96;" width="153" height="96" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F11.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F11.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/01_traj_def_blur_min.png" id="S5.F11.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/97;" width="153" height="97" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F11.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F11.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/csv_traj_before.png" id="S5.F11.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/115;" width="153" height="115" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F11.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F11.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/csv_traj_middle_90.png" id="S5.F11.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/115;" width="153" height="115" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F11.sf5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(e)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F11.sf6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/csv_traj_final_01.png" id="S5.F11.sf6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/115;" width="153" height="115" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F11.sf6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(f)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 11</span>: </span><span id="S5.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Time-lapse and actual position trajectories of the robot using the pretrained policy, the policy during adaptation training at episode 90, and the adapted policy. The pretrained policy is designed for the original task of obstacle avoidance. The adapted policy enables the robot to reach a target position (an additional task) while still avoiding collisions (the original task).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiment Setup.</span> As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F11.sf1" title="In Fig. 11 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(a)</span></a>, the original task of the quadrupedal robot is to avoid collision with the obstacle placed in the center with <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{x}}=-0.5\text{m},p_{\mathrm{y}}=0\text{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{x}}=-0.5\text{m},p_{\mathrm{y}}=0\text{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and radius <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.265\text{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.265</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.265\text{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, for which we define the following predefined stage cost:</p>
<table id="S5.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(t))=\begin{cases}-(p_{\mathrm{x}}^{2}(t)+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t))+100,\quad&amp;\text{if collision},\\
-(p_{\mathrm{x}}^{2}(t)+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t)),\quad&amp;\text{else},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if collision</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>else</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(t))=\begin{cases}-(p_{\mathrm{x}}^{2}(t)+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t))+100,\quad&amp;\text{if collision},\\
-(p_{\mathrm{x}}^{2}(t)+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t)),\quad&amp;\text{else},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the collision means <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)+0.5)^{2}+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t))^{\frac{1}{2}}\leq 0.595" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.595</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)+0.5)^{2}+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t))^{\frac{1}{2}}\leq 0.595</annotation></semantics></math> m. The stage cost <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}(\boldsymbol{x}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E27" title="In V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) is designed to give the robot a penalty when the collision occurs and to encourage the robot to move away from the position <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{x}}=0,p_{\mathrm{y}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{x}}=0,p_{\mathrm{y}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. A pretrained policy <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{\mu}})</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding critic <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{u}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Q}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{u}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\hat{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained using the DDPG algorithm. A demonstration of trajectories generated by <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F12.sf1" title="In Fig. 12 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12(a)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F12" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F12.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/pretrain.svg" id="S5.F12.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/72;" width="153" height="72"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F12.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F12.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.02720v1/task1_training.png" id="S5.F12.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/72;" width="153" height="72" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F12.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F12.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/task1_aftertrain.svg" id="S5.F12.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/72;" width="153" height="72"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F12.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 12</span>: </span><span id="S5.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Demonstration trajectories. Colors indicate training epochs, where lighter colors correspond to later stages in training.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The additional task is to reach the target position <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{x}}=1.5\text{m},p_{\mathrm{y}}=0\text{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{x}}=1.5\text{m},p_{\mathrm{y}}=0\text{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F11.sf1" title="In Fig. 11 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(a)</span></a>. To achieve this, we define the following additional stage cost function:</p>
<table id="S5.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\boldsymbol{x}(t))=\sqrt{(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-1.5)^{2}+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\boldsymbol{x}(t))=\sqrt{(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-1.5)^{2}+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The proposed algorithm is subsequently employed to adapt the original control policy to minimize the cost function in (<a href="#S5.E28" title="In V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>), while ensuring that the adapted policy continues to satisfy the original task constraints. The demonstration trajectories generated by the adapted policy <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\boldsymbol{x}(t),\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F12.sf3" title="In Fig. 12 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12(c)</span></a>, and the corresponding trajectories observed during the training process are presented in Fig. <a href="#S5.F12.sf2" title="In Fig. 12 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12(b)</span></a>. This setup illustrates how the proposed algorithm can effectively adapt a pretrained policy to accommodate an additional objective, provided that the optimal solution sets for the two tasks have a non-empty intersection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results Analysis.</span> As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F11.sf1" title="In Fig. 11 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(a)</span></a>, the robot initially executes the pretrained policy, which was developed solely for obstacle avoidance. While the robot successfully avoids collisions, it fails to reach the target position since goal-reaching was not included in the original task. The actual trajectory of the robot, recorded via a motion capture system, is depicted in Fig. <a href="#S5.F11.sf4" title="In Fig. 11 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(d)</span></a>. Subsequently, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F11.sf2" title="In Fig. 11 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(b)</span></a>, the robot still does not reach the goal at the <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>90</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90</annotation></semantics></math>-th episode of the adaptation training due to the incomplete adaptation process. Nevertheless, the proposed algorithm effectively utilizes information from both the original policy and the additional goal-reaching task to guide the adaptation. The corresponding trajectory is shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F11.sf5" title="In Fig. 11 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(e)</span></a>. After completing the adaptation training, the robot executes the fully adapted policy. As demonstrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F11.sf3" title="In Fig. 11 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(c)</span></a>, the robot successfully reaches the goal without any collisions, thus fulfilling both the original (collision avoidance) and additional (goal-reaching) task objectives. The trajectory for this successful trial is also shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F11.sf5" title="In Fig. 11 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(e)</span></a>. Furthermore, to assess the generalizability of the adapted policy, an additional trial is conducted from a different initial position. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F13" title="Fig. 13 ‣ V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, the robot reaches the goal without collisions, demonstrating the effectiveness of the adapted policy in meeting both original and additional task objectives. Details are available in the supplementary video.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F13.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/03_traj_def-min.png" id="S5.F13.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/233;" width="476" height="233" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F13.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F13.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Time-lapse trajectory with adapted policy.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F13.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/csv_traj_final_03.png" id="S5.F13.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F13.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F13.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Actual trajectory with adapted policy.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 13</span>: </span><span id="S5.F13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Time-lapse and actual position trajectories of the robot using the same adapted policy with a different initial position. The adapted policy enables the robot to reach a target position (additional task) while still avoiding collisions (original task).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we aim to utilize the proposed method to adapt the previously learned policy, which enables the robot to avoid obstacles and reach a specified goal, to additionally account for a new obstacle introduced into the environment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F14" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F14.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/add_01_traj_def-min.png" id="S5.F14.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/276;" width="476" height="276" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F14.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F14.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Time-lapse trajectory with second adapted policy.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F14.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.02720v1/figs/csv_traj_add_01.png" id="S5.F14.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F14.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F14.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Actual trajectory with second adapted policy.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F14.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 14</span>: </span><span id="S5.F14.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Time-lapse and actual position trajectories of the robot using the first adapted policy as the new pretrained policy for the new original task. The new pretrained policy is designed for the new original task of orange obstacle avoidance and goal reaching. The second adapted policy enables the robot to reach a target position while still avoiding collisions with original (orange) and additional (violet) obstacles.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiment Setup.</span> As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F14" title="Fig. 14 ‣ V-B Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, under the same setup as in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>, the new original task for the quadrupedal robot is to avoid collision with the orange obstacle and reach the target position using the resulting policy from Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The additional task is to avoid collision with a newly introduced obstacle located at <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{x}}=0.5\text{m},p_{\mathrm{y}}=0.2\text{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{x}}=0.5\text{m},p_{\mathrm{y}}=0.2\text{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with a radius of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.17\text{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.17</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.17\text{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F14" title="Fig. 14 ‣ V-B Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and marked in violet. Notably, this obstacle is deliberately positioned on the planned trajectories generated by the resulting policy from Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F15.sf1" title="In Fig. 15 ‣ V-B Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15(a)</span></a>. To incorporate this additional task, we define the following stage cost function:</p>
<table id="S5.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\boldsymbol{x}(t))=\begin{cases}\sqrt{(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-1.5)^{2}+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t)}+10,\quad&amp;\text{if collision},\\
\sqrt{(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-1.5)^{2}+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t)},\quad&amp;\text{else},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if collision</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>else</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\boldsymbol{x}(t))=\begin{cases}\sqrt{(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-1.5)^{2}+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t)}+10,\quad&amp;\text{if collision},\\
\sqrt{(p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-1.5)^{2}+p_{\mathrm{y}}^{2}(t)},\quad&amp;\text{else},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the collision here means <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-0.5)^{2}+(p_{\mathrm{y}}(t)-0.2)^{2})^{\frac{1}{2}}\leq 0.42\text{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>0.42</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((p_{\mathrm{x}}(t)-0.5)^{2}+(p_{\mathrm{y}}(t)-0.2)^{2})^{\frac{1}{2}}\leq 0.42\text{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proposed algorithm is subsequently utilized to further adapt the initially modified control policy from Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Policy Adaptation to a Different Target Position ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>, enabling the robot to avoid the newly introduced obstacle. The adaptation process is illustrated through the training trajectories shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F15.sf2" title="In Fig. 15 ‣ V-B Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15(b)</span></a>, while the trajectories generated by the final adapted policy are presented in Fig. <a href="#S5.F15.sf3" title="In Fig. 15 ‣ V-B Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15(c)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F15" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F15.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/task2_notrain.svg" id="S5.F15.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/72;" width="153" height="72"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F15.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F15.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/task2_training.svg" id="S5.F15.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/72;" width="153" height="72"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F15.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F15.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02720v1/task2_aftertrain.svg" id="S5.F15.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/72;" width="153" height="72"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F15.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F15.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 15</span>: </span><span id="S5.F15.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Demonstration trajectories. Colors indicate training epochs, where lighter colors correspond to later stages in training.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results Analysis.</span> Fig. <a href="#S5.F14" title="Fig. 14 ‣ V-B Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> presents the experimental results. After completing training, the robot executes the second adapted policy. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F14.sf1" title="In Fig. 14 ‣ V-B Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14(a)</span></a>, the robot successfully reaches the target position without any collisions, satisfying both the original task (collision avoidance for the orange obstacle and goal reaching) and the additional task (collision avoidance for the violet obstacle). The collision-free robot trajectory is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F14.sf2" title="In Fig. 14 ‣ V-B Policy Adaptation to an Additional Obstacle ‣ V Real-World Demonstrations ‣ A Control-Barrier-Function-Based Algorithm for Policy Adaptation in Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14(b)</span></a>.
Multiple trials with different initial positions, available in the supplementary video, were conducted to evaluate the generalizability. These experiments also highlight that the proposed algorithm supports sequential policy adaptation to multiple additional tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Concluding Remarks</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we have presented a policy adaptation algorithm, termed CBF-PA, for adjusting the parameters of a predesigned policy to minimize an additional task while simultaneously preserving the near-optimal performance of the original task. The key contributions of this work are: (i) formulating the policy adaptation problem as a constrained optimization problem, and (ii) leveraging the constraint satisfaction properties of CBFs to guarantee the fulfillment of the original task while minimizing an additional objective. The main advantages of the proposed CBF-PA approach are threefold. First, the introduction of a CBF with a relaxation variable addresses the potential empty intersection of optimal sets for the original and additional tasks. Second, the proposed algorithm operates as a closed-loop system, thereby reducing computational complexity. Finally, we validate the efficacy of the method through experiments on both low- and high-dimensional systems, demonstrating that CBF-PA outperforms traditional imitation and transfer learning methods by achieving lower additional costs while strictly maintaining the deviation from the optimal cost of the original task throughout the learning process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One shortcoming of the proposed algorithm discussed in this paper is that standard RL algorithms often require the addition of exploration noise to the control policy for improved data exploration. However, this noise may negatively impact the performance of the proposed algorithm during training. Future work could focus on improving the definition of exploration noise within the context of the proposed algorithm. Additionally, the optimal critic and actor in the system may have nonconvex structures, potentially leading to multiple optimal solutions for the proposed methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
